Dear Dr. Symanzik,
	I wanted to write you an anonymous e-mail about the way class is being run.
I wanted to tell you some things that I dislike about the way you run the
class so that maybe future classes will not have this problem (or maybe class
now could be changed).  I don't want to give you my name because I don't want
you to look at or grade my papers in any different way.
	1.  For the amount of time that we have in class, I understand that we have
to get an excessive amount of notes; however, the amount of time we have for
break is not enough.  We all work very hard in that class, trying to follow
you the best that we can, and I think that we deserve more than 10 minutes.
In my other 2 hour-40 minute classes, we have a 15-20 minute break, and they
are not nearly as strenuous/challenging as yours, note-taking wise and
understanding wise.  I think that is only fair to us.  Also, is there any way
that you can avoid giving notes up until the very last second of class?  I
have a 7:20 class, and I am frequently late because I get out of your class
late, and I have to practically run across campus to get to my next class.  I
do not like being late to class -- it makes me feel that the professor feels
that I am disrespectful to her because she constantly gives me "looks" when I
come in late.
	2.  You seem to like solving your own problems.  I know that you are trying
to teach us in the best way that you know how, but I think that people do not
ask questions because you either go too fast for them to even raise their
hands OR that you always say that they can come to you at the end of class.
What you may not realize is that other people may have the same question but
may be too shy to speak up in front of the class.  I know that you have office
hours, and that people can make appointments if they do not understand an
assignment, but with some schedules (such as mine), no time is convenient
outside of class.  Quite a few of the students in the class are adults who
have full-time jobs.  They are unable to make it to any of these times, which
makes answering questions in class essential to their learning.  However,
there are some cases of the younger students who also work during the day when
they are not in class who also can not make it to these times.  One suggestion
would be that you let us, the students, interact with the problems as you
solve them.  This will help you to see if we understand the notes and problems
also.  SLOW DOWN PLEASE!  You are a good teacher -- you just need to slow
down.  This speed is not allowing us to absorb information before we are on to
another topic.  I don't know about you, but my brain is certainly not a
sponge.
	3.  HOMEWORK!  Yes, homework is also essential to our learning.  But for the
point values that the homework is, there are entirely too many problems.  If
you are only going to give us 20 points for doing the assignment, don't treat
it like it is a test.  That is what I feel like every time I sit down to do my
homework.  Also, I have 5 other classes that I am taking that are just as
important, if not more important, than this one.  I am spending so much time
on homework for Statistics that I worry about getting my other work done.
Also, you said at the beginning of the semester that homework is graded on
completion, not correct answers, and that after the first mistake, it would be
graded that way.  However, I have noticed that despite the fact that you have
said this several times, your "grader" has still counted off for incorrect
answers.  This is UNFAIR, and I am receving a 17 or an 18 where I should be
getting a 20!  I work hard to get those answers, even if they turn out wrong
sometimes!  Not receiving this credit on my homework is like not getting paid
for a job I have done.  I am sure you can imagine how frustrating this would
be.
	4.  TESTS!  Thank you, thank you, thank you for allowing us note sheets!  On
the other hand...I realize that since we have this class only once a week for
2 hours and 40 minutes, that we have to take notes and learn notes for the
next homework assignment before we take the test.  However, if you are going
to give us an hour for the test after having taken notes, could you at least
give us 15 minutes of a break on test days if you can't do it on other days?
And can you give us the full hour for the test?  Last class, I was so
frustrated with the test that I couldn't concentrate because I was afraid I
would not have enough time to finish.  I feel that this was one of the reasons
that I did poorly (I am almost certain that I did poorly).  Also, it took me
until the last minute of class -- I had to leave because I have a class at
7:20!  I am also not a very good test-taker, even when I know the material.  I
always get nervous for reasons that I do not know.  It would make it much more
comfortable for everyone if we had a more comfortable time allotment.
	I am sorry about this letter -- I don't mean to sound ungrateful, but I
seriously feel that needed to be said.  I have spoken with several other
students in the class who agree with me on these important points.  I hope you
will consider them with a great amount of thought.

Signed,
An Anonymous Student

